Z||000004||filthyscarecrow||11-18-2001||02:07 AM||||do they have tech support for their products? i just emailed him, so hopefully i'll hear from him soon,but i'm still wondering which is more. i can get an AFC for $200, so hopefully the chip is less. what size injectors are you using 85GT? fuel pump? where did you get teh injectors? like i sed, i'm looking at DSM turbo injectors, they're like 450cc/min (43 lb/hr) so they'd be fine for cranking boost, but i'm not runnign more than 10 PSI, so they might be overkill... theyr'e just cheap. any ideas?||||reg||1
Z||000005||85frankenstein||11-18-2001||01:06 PM||||I'm quite interested in this discussion as well as I have most of the components to do the retrofit. I found ways of tricking the factory computer into giving the results that I need but would much rather have the factory ECM controlling the ignition and Fuel rather than having a couple of "Black Boxes" taking up valuable real estate on my dashboard and engine bay. Couple of questions. <P>1. Does the chip that was burned handle the ignition retard under boost or are you still running an ignition box? Detonation is a horrible thing for pistons!<P>2. How well does the vehicle handle the new fuel map? Any surging under part throttle while the ECM is trying to figure out the best settings based upon o2 sensor output or does the system go into open-loop mode?<P>3. Most importantly how are the readings from the a/f gauge? Is it rich enough for the upper end or does the fuel system lean out?<P><BR>I'm very interested in hearing about what kind of components you used for this. What kind of turbo, PSI of boost, injector size, fuel pressure... You know, that kind of stuff. Like I said, I'm doing a very similar retro and am very interested in hearing about one that is already done and what kind of things to look out for.<P>Thanks!<P>Bob<BR>||||reg||1
